   "text": "The pattern fits a single cubic spinel, mp-1221799 (Mn3Cu2NiO8).\n\n1. **Measured peaks.** Counting the intensity list along the 0.05\u00b0 grid gives a weak peak at about 30.05\u00b0 (intensity about 27). It also gives the strongest peak at about 35.35\u00b0 (intensity 100).\n2. **Spinel match.** Mn3Cu2NiO8 has a primitive rhombohedral cell with a = 5.948 \u00c5 and \u03b1 \u2248 60\u00b0. That corresponds to a cubic spinel cell of a \u2248 5.948 \u00d7 \u221a2 \u2248 8.41 \u00c5. A spinel of this size puts (220) near 30.0\u00b0 and (311) near 35.4\u00b0. The \"weak then dominant\" pair at 30.05\u00b0 and 35.35\u00b0 is the typical spinel fingerprint.\n3. **Other peaks.** There are three further peaks at higher angle, with intensities of about 11.5, 47 and 21 in that order. I did not count their exact positions. I take them to be spinel reflections such as (400), (511) and (440), but this assignment is assumed rather than checked.\n4. **Weak or absent (111).** The spinel (111) reflection near 18.3\u00b0 is not clearly visible above the noise. This is acceptable because (111) is often weak in spinels.\n5. **Main alternative ruled out.** Ba2TbBiO6 (double perovskite, cubic a \u2248 8.71 \u00c5) would put its strongest peak near 29.0\u00b0 and another near 41.4\u00b0. Neither matches the observed dominant peak at 35.35\u00b0.\n\nThe other seven candidates were not checked line by line. No second phase is needed to explain the strong peaks.\n\n{\"present\": [\"mp-1221799\"], \"confidence\": 0.8, \"note\": \"A weak peak at about 30.05 deg and the dominant peak at 35.35 deg index as spinel (220)/(311) for a cubic cell of a \u2248 8.41 \u00c5, matching Mn3Cu2NiO8; higher-angle peaks were not individually located.\"}",
